    "lawCode": "Add Art 6-A §95, Leg L",
    "lawSection": "Legislative Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S291\n\nSPONSOR: TEDISCO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the legislative law, in relation to establishing a\nveterans internship program (V.I.P. NY)\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nTo set aside a percentage of the current Assembly Intern and Youth\nParticipation Program and Senate Student Program positions for veterans.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThis measure will add a new Article (Article 6-A) to the Legislative Law\nand establish opportunities for veterans to participate in existing\ninternship programs based on the following guidelines:\n\nEligible candidates will be honorably discharged veterans who are unem-\nployed or underemployed and have demonstrated an interest in future\nemployment in public service, government or public policy.\n\nInclusion of (but not limited to) a 10 percent set-aside of in each of\nthe current Assembly Youth Participation Program and Senate Student\nProgram for these eligible veterans.\n\nIn the same manner students are recruited for internship programs, the\nAssembly Intern Office and the Senate Intern Office shall coordinate\nwith each of the respective county veteran's service agencies to identi-\nfy and recruit eligible candidates.\n\nV.I.P. NY enrollees receive a stipend equivalent to the stipend awarded\nto graduate students participating in the same internship program.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nHere in the New York State Legislature, we have a great college intern-\nship program where students learn about our state government, but who\nbetter than our veterans and wounded warriors to see the inner workings\nof the legislative process as interns. This program proposes that V.I.P.\nNY enrollees receive a stipend equivalent to the stipend awarded to\ngraduate student participants. There will be no additional cost to\ntaxpayers to implement V.I.P. NY.\n\nAs our country continues to rely on our service men and women to protect\nthe ideals of our country, we cannot forget those veterans who have come\nhome to rind that integrating back into our workforce and communities\nposes many challenges.\n\nAs a Legislature we have always come together when it comes to those\nservice men and women, whether their service was this year, ten years\nago or sixty years ago. The discipline and work ethic instilled in these\nservice men and women will prove to be a valuable asset to the New York\nState Legislature.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n\n01/21/15: S.2182 REFERRED TO VETERANS, HOMELAND SECURITY AND MILITARY\nAFFAIRS\n\n01/06/16: S.2182 REFERRED TO VETERANS, HOMELAND SECURITY AND MILITARY\nAFFAIRS\n\n01/10/17: S.1713 REFERRED TO VETERANS, HOMELAND SECURITY AND MILITARY\nAFFAIRS\n\n01/03/18: S.1713 REFERRED TO VETERANS, HOMELAND SECURITY AND MILITARY\nAFFAIRS\n\n01/09/19: S.386 REFERRED TO VETERANS, HOMELAND SECURITY AND MILITARY\nAFFAIRS\n\n01/08/20: S.386 REFERRED TO VETERANS, HOMELAND SECURITY AND MILITARY\nAFFAIRS\n\n01/06/21: S.228 REFERRED TO INVESTIGATIONS AND GOVERNMENT OPERATIONS\n\n01/19/21: S.228 REPORTED AND COMMITTED TO FINANCE\n\n11/24/21: S.228 AMEND (T) AND RECOMMIT TO FINANCE\n\n11/24/21: S.228 PRINT NUMBER 228A\n\n01/05/22: S.228A REFERRED TO INVESTIGATIONS AND GOVERNMENT OPERATIONS\n\n02/01/22: S.228A REPORTED AND COMMITTED TO FINANCE\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone, Budget Neutral.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
